  ('506d2f47-dfd2-4109-bce2-751ddf3622fb','Located in Centreville, Virginia, Virginia Archery and Rifle Club pairs archery with rifle shooting under one club umbrella, a combination common among older multi-discipline sportsmen''s clubs in the region. Details on its archery-specific setup, whether that''s a target range, field course, or something else, aren''t listed here, so prospective members should contact the club directly to understand what''s actually available and how to join. For archers in Northern Virginia who also have an interest in rifle shooting, or who simply want a club-based option near Centreville, this is worth a direct inquiry before assuming what''s on offer.'),
  ('b4861d2c-3563-4d70-ab35-f984570f2d92','Vital Shot Archery operates as a commercial archery range in Charlotte, North Carolina, one of the more active archery markets in the Southeast given the city''s size and surrounding hunting culture. Specific details on lane count, course type, or amenities aren''t available in this listing, so anyone interested should contact Vital Shot Archery directly to confirm current offerings, hours, and pricing before visiting. As a commercial operation rather than a members-only club, it''s likely set up to accommodate walk-in archers and newcomers, but that''s worth confirming with the range itself rather than assuming based on the name alone.'),
  ('45e3271c-545f-4b69-8c37-e8c9e2357143','VuDu Archery NYC is a volunteer-run archery club based in the Bronx, part of a small but growing scene of urban archery groups serving New York City residents who don''t have easy access to the open land that most archery ranges depend on. Running a club in a dense urban borough is a different challenge than a rural range, often meaning tighter facilities, specific scheduling, or partnership with an indoor space. The listing doesn''t include specifics on hours, course setup, or membership terms, so Bronx-area archers interested in this club should reach out directly to get current details before showing up.'),
  ('622c5a8a-b823-4afe-bc56-6773c0b2100c','Wa-Xo-Be Archers in Monmouth Junction, New Jersey, gives members real range variety: a 3D course for foam-target practice, a separate field archery course, and 20 lanes for standard target shooting. That''s a substantial footprint for a club-style range, enough to support a range day where some members are working field rounds and others are set up on the line at the same time. For Central Jersey archers looking for a club with more than a single-purpose range, the combination of 3D, field, and a 20-lane target setup makes Wa-Xo-Be one of the more fully-equipped options in the area.'),
  ('39ea4c5d-9740-407c-a3dd-e390fc83b774','Wagoner Traditional Archers, based in Wagoner, Oklahoma, signals its focus right in the name: traditional archery, the recurve-and-longbow, no-sights style of shooting that draws a dedicated following distinct from the compound-bow mainstream. As a volunteer-run club rather than a commercial operation, it likely depends on member involvement to maintain its course and schedule shoots, which is common among traditional archery groups nationwide. No further detail on lanes, course type, or hours is available in this listing, so archers in the Wagoner area interested in traditional shooting should contact the club directly for current information.'),
  ('fc43ad5e-3c4e-4701-8f7b-8f72eaac9217','WaKuWa Archery Club Selah is a volunteer-run archery club serving the Yakima, Washington area, with a name pointing to the nearby town of Selah as part of its identity or origin. Central Washington has a solid outdoor and bowhunting culture given its proximity to open range and hunting land, and clubs like this typically function as member-organized shooting grounds rather than pay-per-visit facilities. This listing doesn''t include specifics on course layout, membership process, or scheduled shoot days, so archers around Yakima or Selah interested in joining should reach out to the club directly for current, accurate details.'),
  ('aa178381-499c-45fa-8374-3d606ed8bf4f','Walker River Bowmen takes its name from the river running through the high desert near Yerington, Nevada, where the club is based. As a volunteer-run group, it likely relies on its members to maintain the range and organize shoots rather than operating with paid staff or posted hours, which is typical for rural Nevada archery clubs serving a small but committed local following. No further detail on course type, lane count, or amenities is listed here, so anyone in the Yerington or greater Lyon County area interested in shooting with Walker River Bowmen should contact the club directly for current information.'),
  ('404c169f-15fd-4863-82bc-c4fd0fe93067','Wallhangers Moss Bluff is a commercial archery range operating in the Moss Bluff area near Lake Charles, Louisiana, a name that nods to the trophy bucks ("wallhangers") that Southwest Louisiana bowhunters are chasing each fall. As a commercial rather than membership-only facility, it''s likely set up for walk-in customers and newcomers, though specific lane counts, course type, and hours aren''t included in this listing. Southwest Louisiana has an active deer bowhunting culture, and a range like this typically serves as a practice stop before the season opens. Anyone interested should contact Wallhangers Moss Bluff directly to confirm current details.')
) AS v(id, description)
